WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.290
- [Narrator] Ladies and
gentlemen, boys and girls,

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:05.950
I present to you the world's
largest burrowing clam.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.960
This is the geoduck.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.293
And oh my God, it's an alien.

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:13.000
What the?

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.553
Oh my God, just look at it.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.720
This is a geoduck.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.214
And well, it looks like a, I guess, yeah.

00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:25.183
Anyway.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.720
The geoduck is a type of saltwater clam

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.530
that only comes from this
region in the pacific northwest.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.060
They can burrow themselves
several feet underground,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.570
which makes collecting
them a little difficult.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.930
Isn't that right, Michael?

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.370
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.030
So when we're harvesting geoduck,

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.470
we use low pressure, high volume water.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.320
The water comes in through the wand,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.170
and then it makes the ground soft enough,

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.110
so you can stick your arm down three feet

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.120
to reach the neck of the
geoduck to pull it out.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.750
The majority of the time
what you're looking for

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.290
is these guys sticking
up or these little holes

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.996
and that's how you know where they are.

00:00:57.996 --> 00:01:00.880
- [Narrator] And viola, a geoduck.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.950
- [Michael] This is the belly,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:04.977
the softest part of the
meat of the geoduck.

00:01:04.977 --> 00:01:07.120
This is the neck where the majority

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.570
of the meat comes from, the
two siphons are on the end

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.780
and they're kind of like
humans where, you know,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.080
they're not all the same; it's
just everyone's different.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.840
- [Narrator] The majority
of geoducks farmed here

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.260
will be sent to east Asia,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.810
where they are a sought after delicacy.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.100
But here in Seattle,
they're still a big deal

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.140
and nobody gets more excited
for geoduck than this guy.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.390
So Tom, how do you prepare it?

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.950
- We have put it in chowders
in the past, from time to time,

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.990
but when you come in here to eat geoduck

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.540
at Taylor Shellfish,
you're gonna have it raw.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.830
It's all about accentuating the freshness

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.460
of the geoduck chilled on the plate

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.710
with all those other
great fresh ingredients.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.710
Prepared right, the texture of geoduck

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.020
is what really separates
it from everything else.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.070
You think about it as having the sweetness

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.550
of the scallop, the saltiness of calamari,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.030
and you have a really
excellent combination.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.300
I love eating geoduck.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.990
I'll just slice a bunch,
put it on a plate,

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.950
squirt a little lemon, throw some salt.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.310
I couldn't ask for more.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.110
- [Narrator] And now the most
important part, the taste.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:14.953
Delicious.

